## 4. Explicit cubic static structure factor with geometric error control

For the degree-six cubic torus with even L>=4, let d be the short marked link
and r_c=c-a run over the other five unit directions. For an allowed k define

    A_d(k)=[2 sum_(i=1)^3 cos(k_i)-exp(-ik.d)]/5.

The charge mean in (6) is exp(-ik.a)(A_d(k)-1). The complete static squared
charge amplitude, not just that mean squared, is

    S_d(k)=<rho(k)^*rho(k)>
          =overbar(|exp(-ik.r_c)-1|^2)+|1-exp(-ik.d)|^2
          =4-(4/5)sum_i cos(k_i)-(8/5)cos(k.d).           (8)

The independent sign has zero mean, so its cross term vanishes. Equivalently
combine both terms of the complex variance (6) with the squared full mean.
For small representatives k the controlled geometric expansion is

    S_d(k)=(2/5)|k|^2+(4/5)(k.d)^2+R_d(k),
    |R_d(k)| <= (1/30)sum_i k_i^4+(1/15)(k.d)^4.         (9)

The bound uses |cos x-1+x^2/2|<=|x|^4/24 separately on each cosine. It is a
geometric bound independent of L, not an estimate for a thermodynamic or
continuum dynamical limit. An allowed nonzero k tending to zero requires
varying the torus sizes. The algebraic formula (8) remains exact at every
allowed k on each finite torus.

Uniformly averaging the six marked orientations gives

    S_av(k)=(4/3)sum_i(1-cos(k_i)),
    |S_av(k)-(2/3)|k|^2| <= (1/18)sum_i k_i^4.           (10)

The all-sign average is essential in (8)-(10). Resolved signs instead have
the exact law of Section3 and generally different static second moments.
Their equal average reproduces these formulas. The degree-three cube obeys
Sections1-3 but not the degree-six coefficients in (8)-(10).

This static charge law is computed before any fitting. Nevertheless it is
